1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,443
For a while, the front doors of Galveston
opened onto empty air.

2
00:00:04,952 --> 00:00:08,176
Beneath the houses stood forests of wooden
supports.

3
00:00:08,610 --> 00:00:12,443
Above the muddy ground, narrow walkways
led people home.

4
00:00:12,952 --> 00:00:15,110
This was not the storm itself.

5
00:00:15,619 --> 00:00:17,395
This was the rebuilding.

6
00:00:17,829 --> 00:00:20,443
And it began with an extraordinary
question:

7
00:00:20,952 --> 00:00:25,167
how do you save a city when the ground
beneath it is too low?

8
00:00:25,981 --> 00:00:28,965
At the turn of the twentieth century,
Galveston was one

9
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:31,414
of the busiest ports in the American
South.

10
00:00:31,848 --> 00:00:33,624
Cotton moved through its docks.

11
00:00:33,905 --> 00:00:35,910
Trains brought goods and visitors.

12
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:38,957
Ships connected Texas to the wider world.

13
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:42,919
But Galveston had a weakness that could
not be seen on a ledger.

14
00:00:43,429 --> 00:00:47,795
The city stood on a long, low barrier
island off the Texas coast.

15
00:00:48,229 --> 00:00:51,480
The highest natural ground on the island
was estimated to be

16
00:00:51,505 --> 00:00:53,662
less than nine feet above sea level.

17
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:59,071
The sea was part of its prosperity, but it
was also close enough to become a threat.

18
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:05,014
In June 1900, the census counted almost
thirty-eight thousand people in the city.

19
00:01:05,448 --> 00:01:07,452
Families lived in wooden houses.

20
00:01:07,657 --> 00:01:09,129
Children walked to school.

21
00:01:09,410 --> 00:01:11,262
Shopkeepers opened their doors.

22
00:01:11,619 --> 00:01:15,910
For most of them, September 8 began like
another day on the Gulf Coast.

23
00:01:16,724 --> 00:01:19,871
Days earlier, a tropical storm had crossed
Cuba.

24
00:01:20,229 --> 00:01:22,614
It strengthened as it moved across the
Gulf.

25
00:01:22,971 --> 00:01:25,433
At the time, there were no weather
satellites,

26
00:01:25,638 --> 00:01:29,194
no radar maps on a phone, and no way for
forecasters

27
00:01:29,219 --> 00:01:33,433
to follow a hurricane over open water with
anything like modern precision.

28
00:01:34,057 --> 00:01:38,462
Reports came from scattered observations,
ships, and telegraph stations.

29
00:01:38,895 --> 00:01:42,729
A storm warning flag was raised in
Galveston on September 7.

30
00:01:43,086 --> 00:01:45,700
The danger was not entirely unknown.

31
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:49,814
But many residents did not expect the
catastrophe that was approaching.

32
00:01:50,248 --> 00:01:53,346
Some had experienced earlier storms and
believed their

33
00:01:53,371 --> 00:01:54,919
city could withstand another.

34
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:59,948
Isaac Cline, the local Weather Bureau
official, watched conditions change.

35
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:04,138
The next day the sea was rising, the wind
was strengthening,

36
00:02:04,305 --> 00:02:07,490
and the information reaching the public
could not show the full

37
00:02:07,543 --> 00:02:08,862
scale of what was coming.

38
00:02:09,524 --> 00:02:13,433
On the morning of September 8, water began
creeping into the streets.

39
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:16,176
Then the Gulf pushed farther inland.

40
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:19,823
By afternoon, people who had expected bad
weather were

41
00:02:19,848 --> 00:02:21,814
confronting something far worse.

42
00:02:22,248 --> 00:02:25,471
The water was not simply rain collecting
in low places.

43
00:02:25,752 --> 00:02:28,976
The hurricane drove a massive surge across
the island.

44
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:33,167
The storm surge was later estimated at
nearly sixteen feet.

45
00:02:33,638 --> 00:02:38,500
Waves carried timbers, pieces of houses,
and everything else they could tear loose.

46
00:02:38,933 --> 00:02:42,157
As debris struck buildings, some walls
gave way.

47
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:45,281
Other structures were lifted from their
foundations.

48
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,167
People climbed to upper floors and
gathered in stronger buildings.

49
00:02:49,371 --> 00:02:51,071
Families took in neighbors.

50
00:02:51,429 --> 00:02:55,033
One building that survived was the Gresham
family's stone house,

51
00:02:55,238 --> 00:02:57,319
known today as Bishop's Palace.

52
00:02:57,752 --> 00:03:00,775
According to accounts of survivors, more
than two hundred

53
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,500
residents found refuge there.

54
00:03:02,895 --> 00:03:07,452
Yet height offered little certainty as
water and wreckage moved through the city.

55
00:03:07,733 --> 00:03:10,604
At the Weather Bureau, the wind gauge
recorded gusts

56
00:03:10,629 --> 00:03:14,233
of roughly one hundred miles an hour
before it blew away.

57
00:03:14,819 --> 00:03:17,205
Conditions grew more violent into the
evening.

58
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:20,481
Darkness made it harder to see where the
water ended

59
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:22,386
or what was coming toward a house.

60
00:03:22,705 --> 00:03:25,357
Cline and his family struggled to survive.

61
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:27,490
His wife died in the storm.

62
00:03:27,886 --> 00:03:31,300
Their loss was one among thousands across
Galveston,

63
00:03:31,543 --> 00:03:34,195
and many families would never be whole
again.

64
00:03:34,552 --> 00:03:38,462
Near midnight, the wind and water finally
began to subside.

65
00:03:38,857 --> 00:03:42,119
What daylight revealed was a city
transformed.

66
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,557
Streets were buried beneath wreckage.

67
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,452
Houses had disappeared or been torn apart.

68
00:03:47,886 --> 00:03:52,252
Survivors searched for relatives among
unfamiliar piles of broken timber.

69
00:03:52,610 --> 00:03:56,062
The death toll could never be counted with
complete certainty.

70
00:03:56,114 --> 00:03:58,805
At least six thousand people died.

71
00:03:58,971 --> 00:04:01,880
Some estimates are considerably higher,
and the storm

72
00:04:01,905 --> 00:04:05,156
remains the deadliest weather-related
natural disaster

73
00:04:05,181 --> 00:04:07,086
in United States history.

74
00:04:07,111 --> 00:04:11,505
The city faced an immediate task as
terrible as the storm itself:

75
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:14,348
caring for the living and finding the
dead.

76
00:04:14,654 --> 00:04:16,534
Temporary morgues were established.

77
00:04:16,559 --> 00:04:19,975
As the scale of the losses became clear,
recovery workers

78
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,934
struggled with more bodies than the city
could handle.

79
00:04:22,959 --> 00:04:26,565
Aid came from outside, but the future was
uncertain.

80
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:29,662
Galveston could rebuild its homes and
businesses.

81
00:04:29,714 --> 00:04:32,710
Yet if another hurricane drove water over
the island,

82
00:04:32,762 --> 00:04:35,605
the same low ground would be waiting
beneath them.

83
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:38,604
Rebuilding also meant living with
uncertainty.

84
00:04:38,629 --> 00:04:42,261
A replacement house could be swept away by
a future surge.

85
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:46,045
Businesses needed a functioning port,
families needed homes,

86
00:04:46,070 --> 00:04:49,753
and the island needed a way to face the
water that surrounded it.

87
00:04:49,778 --> 00:04:53,130
The question was no longer simply how to
restore the city.

88
00:04:53,155 --> 00:04:56,127
It was how to give it a better chance next
time.

89
00:04:56,152 --> 00:04:59,785
A board of engineers proposed two
connected defenses.

90
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:02,576
First came a seawall along the Gulf side:

91
00:05:02,705 --> 00:05:07,023
a massive barrier designed to meet the
waves before they reached the streets.

92
00:05:07,048 --> 00:05:09,967
But a wall alone would leave a problem
behind it.

93
00:05:10,019 --> 00:05:12,737
Much of the city still lay close to sea
level.

94
00:05:12,762 --> 00:05:16,089
The second part of the plan was almost
harder to imagine.

95
00:05:16,114 --> 00:05:17,994
They would raise the ground.

96
00:05:18,019 --> 00:05:21,499
And before the ground could rise,
buildings standing on it

97
00:05:21,524 --> 00:05:22,843
would have to rise too.

98
00:05:23,352 --> 00:05:27,033
Work on the first section of the seawall
began in 1902.

99
00:05:27,467 --> 00:05:31,300
By 1904, a concrete barrier about three
point three

100
00:05:31,429 --> 00:05:33,662
miles long stood along the Gulf.

101
00:05:34,019 --> 00:05:37,471
Its top rose seventeen feet above mean low
water.

102
00:05:37,829 --> 00:05:41,129
Later extensions would carry the wall
farther along the coast.

103
00:05:41,676 --> 00:05:44,889
While the seawall was being built, the
grade-raising project

104
00:05:44,914 --> 00:05:47,757
turned neighborhoods into construction
sites behind it.

105
00:05:48,038 --> 00:05:50,424
The work moved from section to section.

106
00:05:50,743 --> 00:05:54,032
Crews lifted buildings on hand-operated
screw jacks

107
00:05:54,057 --> 00:05:56,290
and supported them on temporary timbers.

108
00:05:56,724 --> 00:05:59,033
About two thousand buildings were raised,

109
00:05:59,276 --> 00:06:01,929
according to the American Society of Civil
Engineers.

110
00:06:02,210 --> 00:06:04,671
That number tells only part of the story.

111
00:06:04,990 --> 00:06:09,090
A building could not simply be set aside
while a new foundation was prepared.

112
00:06:09,295 --> 00:06:13,205
It had to remain supported as crews worked
below and around it.

113
00:06:13,524 --> 00:06:16,813
And every raised house stood among streets
and services

114
00:06:16,838 --> 00:06:18,767
that needed to connect at a new level.

115
00:06:19,048 --> 00:06:22,718
Water pipes, fire hydrants, and streetcar
tracks also had

116
00:06:22,743 --> 00:06:25,395
to be dealt with as the level of the
ground changed.

117
00:06:25,676 --> 00:06:28,367
This was not a model on an engineer's
desk.

118
00:06:28,571 --> 00:06:31,823
It was a living city, full of structures
that had to be

119
00:06:31,848 --> 00:06:35,048
kept in place while the land around them
was remade.

120
00:06:35,073 --> 00:06:38,985
To bring in the material, a canal was cut
through part of Galveston.

121
00:06:39,010 --> 00:06:42,310
Dredges collected sand and carried it
through the canal.

122
00:06:42,362 --> 00:06:45,662
Workers then pumped fill into the areas
being raised,

123
00:06:45,917 --> 00:06:48,280
including spaces beneath the lifted
buildings.

124
00:06:48,305 --> 00:06:50,642
The amount varied from place to place.

125
00:06:50,667 --> 00:06:54,271
The aim was not to lift every street to
exactly the same height,

126
00:06:54,476 --> 00:06:58,081
but to create a higher, sloping grade
behind the seawall.

127
00:06:58,133 --> 00:07:00,671
What looked like chaos had a purpose:

128
00:07:00,724 --> 00:07:05,042
sand gradually became the new ground on
which the city would stand.

129
00:07:05,067 --> 00:07:08,318
Once an area was filled to its new grade,
foundations could

130
00:07:08,343 --> 00:07:10,299
be finished beneath the structures.

131
00:07:10,324 --> 00:07:12,915
Work could then move on to another
section.

132
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:17,662
The change was gradual, not a single
dramatic lift of the entire island.

133
00:07:17,752 --> 00:07:20,965
Some residents continued living in their
houses during the work.

134
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:25,510
To get around, they used makeshift
boardwalks raised above the muddy fill.

135
00:07:25,562 --> 00:07:28,737
Imagine stepping out of a front door onto
a narrow wooden

136
00:07:28,762 --> 00:07:31,886
path while crews reshaped the ground
below.

137
00:07:31,911 --> 00:07:34,604
The transformation also came at a cost.

138
00:07:34,629 --> 00:07:37,804
Taxpayers helped finance the work, and
property owners had

139
00:07:37,829 --> 00:07:39,708
to pay for lifting their buildings.

140
00:07:39,733 --> 00:07:42,832
As streets and sidewalks disappeared
beneath fill,

141
00:07:42,857 --> 00:07:45,880
residents faced the expense of replacing
them too.

142
00:07:45,905 --> 00:07:48,367
The engineering solution was remarkable,

143
00:07:48,648 --> 00:07:52,100
but rebuilding placed real burdens on the
people who remained.

144
00:07:52,419 --> 00:07:55,757
One of the most striking challenges was
Saint Patrick's Church.

145
00:07:56,038 --> 00:07:59,338
The building weighed an estimated three
thousand tons.

146
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:02,233
According to the Galveston and Texas
History Center,

147
00:08:02,438 --> 00:08:05,967
workers used seven hundred jacks to raise
it five feet.

148
00:08:06,248 --> 00:08:09,765
A heavy masonry church, moved upward not
by a single

149
00:08:09,790 --> 00:08:14,348
enormous machine, but by the patient
coordination of many smaller lifts.

150
00:08:14,705 --> 00:08:16,862
The main work continued for years.

151
00:08:17,333 --> 00:08:20,443
Temporary supports disappeared beneath new
foundations.

152
00:08:20,648 --> 00:08:24,481
The temporary canal was filled back in.
Streets were rebuilt.

153
00:08:24,838 --> 00:08:28,394
Little by little, the strange city of
boardwalks and raised

154
00:08:28,419 --> 00:08:30,957
houses gave way to a more familiar one.

155
00:08:31,390 --> 00:08:34,919
Then another hurricane put the defenses to
the test.

156
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:38,652
In 1915, a powerful storm struck the
region.

157
00:08:38,933 --> 00:08:42,032
Galveston suffered damage, but the loss of
life in the city

158
00:08:42,057 --> 00:08:44,138
was far lower than in 1900.

159
00:08:44,457 --> 00:08:47,643
The seawall and raised ground had become
part of a broader

160
00:08:47,848 --> 00:08:51,148
response to a danger the city now
understood much better.

161
00:08:51,619 --> 00:08:53,738
That did not make Galveston invulnerable.

162
00:08:54,095 --> 00:08:57,624
Hurricanes continued to threaten the
island, and in 2008

163
00:08:57,752 --> 00:09:01,129
Hurricane Ike brought a major surge to the
Texas coast.

164
00:09:01,486 --> 00:09:05,270
No wall can erase the sea or guarantee
that every future

165
00:09:05,295 --> 00:09:07,376
storm will be survived without loss.

166
00:09:07,695 --> 00:09:10,957
But the people of Galveston had done
something extraordinary.

167
00:09:11,390 --> 00:09:14,157
After a disaster that destroyed thousands
of lives,

168
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:16,595
they chose not only to rebuild their city.

169
00:09:16,914 --> 00:09:18,805
They changed the ground beneath it.

170
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:22,794
For years, homes stood above muddy streets
while workers

171
00:09:22,819 --> 00:09:25,814
brought in the sand that would make a
different future possible.

172
00:09:26,095 --> 00:09:29,243
The storm had revealed how vulnerable
Galveston was.

173
00:09:29,524 --> 00:09:34,403
The answer was written, piece by piece,
into the shape of the city itself.
